WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:03.629
Lara Hawketts and her husband live with their
kids in a two-story house

00:00:03.629 --> 00:00:06.320
in the Forest Hills neighborhood of Washington,
D.C.

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:11.080
In 2009, Hawketts lost her job working for
a British consulting firm.

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:13.180
Looking for ways to pick up extra income,

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:16.919
Hawketts discovered that she could host short-term
guests for a modest fee

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:18.940
through a new service called Airbnb.

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:20.880
So they would stay in our basement

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.260
where they would, one person will be on the
sofa,

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:25.320
two people would be in the queen bed.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:30.020
We'd have a pullout couch and a day bed and
the whole family would just squish up

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:31.020
and they didn't care.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:33.420
And the experience they got from that was
so different

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.720
to something you would get in a hotel.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:39.660
Airbnb has seen explosive growth in Washington,
D.C.,

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.640
which is among the nation’s most transient
cities.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:46.240
As we started meeting neighbors and friends
who were like,

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:48.740
wow, this is so cool, it's been so great for
you guys.

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:49.950
“How do we do it?

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:51.980
Oh, we don't want to get involved in all of
that.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:55.980
So, along with her husband, Hawketts started
Home Sweet City,

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:59.440
a business managing Airbnb rentals for other
hosts.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:02.600
Today they oversee more than 60 properties...

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:06.320
And we just hear these amazing stories of
folks that would come to the city,

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:10.780
save for sometimes their entire lives, bring
their family

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:12.900
and it be there one trip of a lifetime to
DC

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.340
and there was no way they could afford to
stay in a hotel.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:22.280
Today, a mid-scale hotel room in Washington
D.C. runs on average $237 per night.

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:27.100
For the about the same price you can rent
this centrally located four bedroom historic townhouse,

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:32.140
or for $110 this modern basement apartment
near Capitol Hill.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:39.560
Or, for travelers on a budget, there’s this
twin bed in a shared room near Dulles airport for $16.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.000
Hearing no further discussion,

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:45.450
all those in favor of the bill say aye, aye,
opposed...

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:48.350
it passes unanimously.

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:51.680
On November 13th, with the support of the
hotel Worker’s Union

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:53.300
and the industry’s trade group,

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:55.160
the DC city council passed a bill

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:58.900
that could have a devastating impact on the
district’s short-term rental market.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:03.540
Airbnb hosts can no longer rent out properties
in which they don’t reside.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:08.320
Those renting out their own spare rooms and
couches will be required to obtain a license,

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:11.380
report their activities to the city on a monthly
basis,

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:15.780
and be present during a stay, with the exception
of up to 90 days a year.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:19.220
City Council Chairman Phil Mendelson says
the intent of the bill

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:22.600
is to eliminate competition from airbnb rentals,

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:26.340
and make it cheaper for permanent residents
to live in Washington, D.C.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:31.220
So we limit the short term rentals to a person's
primary residence

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:35.920
and prohibited to a second or third property…

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:41.600
We think that that correlates to improving
the housing supply in terms of affordability.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:46.500
But there’s a danger this new bill could
unintentionally wipe out the Airbnb market all together.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:51.220
As the council has started to look at short
term housing as a market

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:53.180
and what the issues might be,

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:59.620
there's been a little bit of a rush to regulate
without realizing that D.C.s current zoning

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:05.160
actually prohibits short term housing in most
zones.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:09.040
Tracy Loh, a data scientist at George Washington
University,

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:12.700
points out that even though most Airbnb rentals
are already illegal,

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:15.280
the City has never enforced the zoning code.

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:17.100
This new law could change that.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:20.080
If the city gets involved in regulating airbnb

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:26.120
and requires the platform to register, get
a permit and share data,

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:32.080
then they will know where airbnb activity
is happening in the city

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:36.560
and that means that they'll know where there
are code violations.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:43.300
If the zoning part of that bill, um, restricts
us from renting a basement here in this way,

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.240
we probably won't be able to stay in this
house.

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:49.650
The fact that we rent our basement so frequently,
it pays two thirds of our mortgage

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:52.600
All 13 members have asked the zoning commission
please,

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:56.860
In fact, on an emergency basis, we'd like
you to revise their zoning regulations

00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:01.120
to permit short term rentals in a person's
primary residence.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:03.420
Some of our owners have actually just said
woah

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:09.260
you know the ones that are a little bit nervous
of the situation and want to plan ahead.

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:12.840
They've said 'okay we're going to stop this
and we're going to get a long term tenant.'

00:04:12.849 --> 00:04:16.239
But it's very few, a couple have gone that
route early,

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:20.180
even before it's passed or anything's passed.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:21.900
And some are completely the opposite,

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:25.349
I'm going to do this whether they try and
stop me or not, you know?

00:04:25.349 --> 00:04:27.700
I'm just going to take the risk and run with
it.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:31.880
Chairman Mendelson says that airbnb hosts
should embrace the new law

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:38.240
because, assuming the zoning code changes,
it provides a legal pathway for them to operate.

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:42.460
There does need to be some minimum level of
regulation

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:45.139
with regard to a kind of transient accommodation

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:47.960
There had been complaints about party houses

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:52.960
there had been complaints about how there's
all this transient activity,

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:55.251
but the property owner isn't there.

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:59.040
There's not a person that has an investment
in the community.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:04.300
Mister McDuffie's bill made me feel like I'm
back in the USSR.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:07.340
The bill would make it hard if not impossible

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:11.680
for me to maintain my home of almost 25 years
in D.C.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:15.960
I've bumped into a lot of small business owners
and they're bringing in their letters

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:19.780
And they're saying 'look I run this business,
I depend on this additional income,'

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:23.960
So there's so many stories like that that
it just can't be a bad thing,

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:26.360
We created a company out of necessity

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:30.100
and out of sheer determination and blood,
sweat and tears,

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:33.560
we built it from nothing in a new market,

00:05:33.569 --> 00:05:38.139
in something that has never been done before
in an urban market.

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:40.700
And here we are and it feels like it's going
to be taken away from us.

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:45.080
And our business might have to change but
we're gonna sure as hell try and keep it going.

